00:00:26.970 --> 00:00:28.710
and it sucked and I wondered,

00:00:28.710 --> 00:00:31.890
what's the point of
having all of these apps

00:00:31.890 --> 00:00:33.360
if they don't do anything, right?

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:36.240
This is not the Uber that I used to know

00:00:36.240 --> 00:00:39.510
nor the one that inspired
hundreds of businesses

00:00:39.510 --> 00:00:41.820
billing themselves as Uber for laundry,

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:44.130
Uber for restaurants, Uber for toilets.

00:00:44.130 --> 00:00:45.510
These companies, of course,

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:46.860
make up the gig economy

00:00:46.860 --> 00:00:49.080
and they use slick intuitive apps

00:00:49.080 --> 00:00:50.520
to organize contract workers

00:00:50.520 --> 00:00:54.360
who can make all your
dreams come true for cheap.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.970
Yet those promises have
become more suspect

00:00:56.970 --> 00:00:59.190
as consumers face longer wait times

00:00:59.190 --> 00:01:01.470
and higher costs for services.

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:03.030
This all has us wondering,

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:05.340
was the gig economy a farce built

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:07.500
on an unsustainable financial model?

00:01:07.500 --> 00:01:11.280
Why are some of the most
successful gig startups struggling?

00:01:11.280 --> 00:01:14.550
How has it become such a
dominant force in American labor

00:01:14.550 --> 00:01:18.690
even as its flagship
companies continue to tank?

00:01:18.690 --> 00:01:20.610
Let's find out in this Wisecrack Edition

