00:03:08.940 --> 00:03:11.370
And now back to the show.

00:03:11.370 --> 00:03:14.220
Now my little ride share
anecdote, as you know,

00:03:14.220 --> 00:03:16.080
is become the norm.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:18.637
As Derek Thompson's recent
piece in The Atlantic put it,

00:03:18.637 --> 00:03:21.120
"Something beyond rising
energy and labor costs

00:03:21.120 --> 00:03:24.840
is leading to sticker shock on
once-cheap urban amenities."

00:03:24.840 --> 00:03:26.370
Clearly something has changed

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:29.910
since Airbnb first hit the scene in 2008.

00:03:29.910 --> 00:03:31.710
This was the perfect
moment for the gig economy

00:03:31.710 --> 00:03:33.360
to make its grand debut,

00:03:33.360 --> 00:03:36.330
as the US was reeling
from the Great Recession.

00:03:36.330 --> 00:03:37.890
The unemployment rate had doubled

00:03:37.890 --> 00:03:41.100
and other workers faced
furloughs and reduced hours.

00:03:41.100 --> 00:03:43.380
This made alternatives
to classic employment

00:03:43.380 --> 00:03:45.120
seem quiet appealing,

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.340
like a potential safety net.

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:50.220
As economist Juliet Schor
writes in "After the Gig,"

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:52.290
in the years following 2008

00:03:52.290 --> 00:03:54.750
people came to believe
that digital technology

00:03:54.750 --> 00:03:56.940
could solve the problem of work.

00:03:56.940 --> 00:04:00.090
The thinking went that
algorithms and crowdsourcing

00:04:00.090 --> 00:04:01.320
could do the work of bosses

00:04:01.320 --> 00:04:04.890
while software could
reorganize economic activity

00:04:04.890 --> 00:04:06.900
into a person-to-person structure.

00:04:06.900 --> 00:04:09.900
In this way she notes that
"this empowers individuals

00:04:09.900 --> 00:04:12.120
to take control of their lives.

00:04:12.120 --> 00:04:15.240
Vast swathes of the economy,
especially in services,

00:04:15.240 --> 00:04:17.580
are ripe for this transformation.

00:04:17.580 --> 00:04:21.390
This vision came to be
called the sharing economy."

00:04:21.390 --> 00:04:24.150
Note that we're gonna
focus on the United States

00:04:24.150 --> 00:04:26.670
and we're going by scholar Luigi Zingales'

00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:28.290
definition of the gig economy

00:04:28.290 --> 00:04:30.420
as any time you have "a digital platform

00:04:30.420 --> 00:04:33.300
that coordinates a large
amount of people doing a job.

00:04:33.300 --> 00:04:35.430
It's the case of Uber,
it's the case of Lyft,

00:04:35.430 --> 00:04:37.920
it's the case of Airbnb,
it's the case of Fiverr,

00:04:37.920 --> 00:04:40.830
it's the case of the
Mechanical Turks on Amazon."

00:04:40.830 --> 00:04:44.370
And this model changed the very
idea of what labor could be.

00:04:44.370 --> 00:04:46.860
It seemed to offer freedom and flexibility

00:04:46.860 --> 00:04:49.380
without compromising wages via the magic

00:04:49.380 --> 00:04:51.630
of part-time of gig-based work.

00:04:51.630 --> 00:04:53.910
For folks without spare rooms to rent out,

00:04:53.910 --> 00:04:55.800
Uber launched in 2009,

00:04:55.800 --> 00:04:57.630
followed by Fiverr in 2010,

00:04:57.630 --> 00:04:59.028
Rover in 2011,

00:04:59.028 --> 00:05:00.960
Instacart in 2012 and beyond.

00:05:00.960 --> 00:05:03.450
There was a gig for
seemingly every skill set.

00:05:03.450 --> 00:05:06.750
Whether your specialty was
picking the ripest avocado,

00:05:06.750 --> 00:05:08.880
bagging the (beep) of a stranger's dog,

00:05:08.880 --> 00:05:11.130
or copy-editing somebody's novel.

00:05:11.130 --> 00:05:13.320
Plus, you could hypothetically
make your own hours

00:05:13.320 --> 00:05:15.030
and be your own boss

00:05:15.030 --> 00:05:17.040
with your earning potential limited only

00:05:17.040 --> 00:05:19.590
by your willingness to work hard.

00:05:19.590 --> 00:05:21.060
This caught on fast.

00:05:21.060 --> 00:05:22.950
By 2016, the Pew Research Center found

00:05:22.950 --> 00:05:25.320
that nearly 25% of American adults

00:05:25.320 --> 00:05:27.120
were making money from gig apps.

00:05:27.120 --> 00:05:29.550
And the startup founders behind the apps

00:05:29.550 --> 00:05:32.850
preached the gospel of
Silicon Valley utopianism,

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:35.010
promising staggering innovation.

00:05:35.010 --> 00:05:38.250
Like Uber's grand plans
for self-driving cars

00:05:38.250 --> 00:05:42.060
or Amazon delivering your
year supply of floss by drone,

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:44.640
it would be a brand new economy.

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:46.500
At first, it seemed to be working.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:48.450
That is, if you didn't follow the money.

00:05:48.450 --> 00:05:50.790
If you're paying $7 for that ride,

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:53.970
how is your Uber driver
making a living wage?

00:05:53.970 --> 00:05:55.770
Well, A, they probably weren't,

00:05:55.770 --> 00:05:57.900
and B, the baffling bankroll

00:05:57.900 --> 00:05:59.610
was thanks to venture capitalists

00:05:59.610 --> 00:06:02.970
who allowed gig economy
apps to operate at a loss.

00:06:02.970 --> 00:06:05.820
This was based on the
assumption that in the future

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:09.360
when they've cornered the market
on, say, self-driving cars,

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:11.910
that's when they'll really see a profit.

00:06:11.910 --> 00:06:15.750
In the meantime people got
used to the idea of $7 rides.

00:06:15.750 --> 00:06:16.957
As Thompson writes,

00:06:16.957 --> 00:06:19.080
"For the past decade, people like me,

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:21.180
youngish, urbanish, professionalish,

00:06:21.180 --> 00:06:23.100
got a sweetheart deal from Uber,

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:26.250
the Uber-for-X clones
that vaguely pretended

00:06:26.250 --> 00:06:27.330
to be tech companies.

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:29.760
Almost each time you or I ordered a pizza

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:30.720
or hailed a taxi,

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:33.150
the company behind that app lost money.

00:06:33.150 --> 00:06:35.790
In effect, these startups,
backed by venture capital,

00:06:35.790 --> 00:06:37.620
were paying us, the consumers,

00:06:37.620 --> 00:06:39.450
to buy their products."

00:06:39.450 --> 00:06:41.070
But are those glory days over

00:06:41.070 --> 00:06:42.780
if consumers are no longer getting

00:06:42.780 --> 00:06:45.120
too-good-to-be-true prices?

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:49.350
Because the gig economy has
always run on underpaid labor.

00:06:49.350 --> 00:06:50.670
And to understand this

00:06:50.670 --> 00:06:54.120
we need to contextualize where
gig as we know it came from.

00:06:54.120 --> 00:06:56.040
As scholar Louis Hyman explains,

00:06:56.040 --> 00:06:57.840
the decades following the New Deal

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:01.410
witnessed the golden age of
stable, long-term employment

00:07:01.410 --> 00:07:03.660
when union membership was skyrocketing

00:07:03.660 --> 00:07:06.150
and companies competing for top talent

00:07:06.150 --> 00:07:08.280
started offering things
like generous wages

00:07:08.280 --> 00:07:10.140
and employer-based healthcare.

00:07:10.140 --> 00:07:12.810
But after the economic boom in the 1950s,

00:07:12.810 --> 00:07:15.810
corporate structures started to change.

00:07:15.810 --> 00:07:18.420
At the top, short-term consultants

00:07:18.420 --> 00:07:19.920
started replacing executives,

00:07:19.920 --> 00:07:22.650
while at the bottom union
workers were replaced

00:07:22.650 --> 00:07:24.600
by cheaper day laborers.

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:26.430
And while these moves helped businesses

00:07:26.430 --> 00:07:29.340
seem lean and therefore more profitable,

00:07:29.340 --> 00:07:31.500
this also decreased job security.

00:07:31.500 --> 00:07:33.150
This, as Hyman argues,

00:07:33.150 --> 00:07:34.560
was indicative of a sea change

00:07:34.560 --> 00:07:35.970
in how corporations function.

00:07:35.970 --> 00:07:38.820
They went from primarily
trying to minimize risk

00:07:38.820 --> 00:07:41.940
to primarily trying to maximize profits.

00:07:41.940 --> 00:07:44.460
It wasn't about keeping
your family company running

00:07:44.460 --> 00:07:46.680
and making quality goods for decades.

00:07:46.680 --> 00:07:48.960
It was about making bank today.

00:07:48.960 --> 00:07:52.410
And this was a whole new
way of looking at business.

00:07:52.410 --> 00:07:54.960
The risk-taking entrepreneur

00:07:54.960 --> 00:07:57.600
thus became the capitalist ideal.

00:07:57.600 --> 00:08:00.810
And in pursuit of stand-alone
unabashed profits,

00:08:00.810 --> 00:08:03.180
short-term returns became the new goal.

00:08:03.180 --> 00:08:06.000
And one way to make
sure your quarterly ROI

00:08:06.000 --> 00:08:07.800
looks shiny and efficient

00:08:07.800 --> 00:08:09.690
is to hire flexible laborers

00:08:09.690 --> 00:08:12.690
who take up less of your
operating cost overall.

00:08:12.690 --> 00:08:15.030
The tech industry of the '70s and '80s

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:16.860
relied on this business model.

00:08:16.860 --> 00:08:19.740
Short-term investments partnered
with flexible production

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:22.200
to great growth and success.

00:08:22.200 --> 00:08:23.910
It also, as Hyman points out,

00:08:23.910 --> 00:08:26.340
relied upon the use of undocumented labor

00:08:26.340 --> 00:08:28.200
at an industrial scale

00:08:28.200 --> 00:08:30.090
by the way of sub-contractors

00:08:30.090 --> 00:08:33.420
who were paid on a per-project basis.

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:35.760
The rise of the internet
would greatly influence

00:08:35.760 --> 00:08:38.010
how work is sourced and conducted,

00:08:38.010 --> 00:08:40.020
making it even easier
to source temp workers

00:08:40.020 --> 00:08:42.180
via Craigslist or apps.

00:08:42.180 --> 00:08:44.040
No agency necessary.

00:08:44.040 --> 00:08:47.790
These workers would be known
as independent contractors.

00:08:47.790 --> 00:08:50.640
There are big benefits to
digitally sourcing contractors,

00:08:50.640 --> 00:08:53.407
as Jamie Woodcock and Mark
Graham explain in their book

00:08:53.407 --> 00:08:54.360
"The Gig Economy."

00:08:54.360 --> 00:08:57.330
For one, it adds a layer of
invisibility and isolation

00:08:57.330 --> 00:08:59.910
that obscures the people doing the work.

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:01.710
It's hard to know how
many colleagues you have

00:09:01.710 --> 00:09:03.240
at TaskRabbit, after all,

00:09:03.240 --> 00:09:05.490
if there is no way to
communicate with them.

00:09:05.490 --> 00:09:06.780
And for many of these apps,

00:09:06.780 --> 00:09:09.000
the sheer number of willing contractors

00:09:09.000 --> 00:09:11.460
has created an over-supply of labor.

00:09:11.460 --> 00:09:12.877
Woodcock and Graham write,

00:09:12.877 --> 00:09:14.970
"As a result of this oversupply,

00:09:14.970 --> 00:09:17.490
individual workers have very little power

00:09:17.490 --> 00:09:20.160
to negotiate wages or working conditions."

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:21.750
Which is why workers way back when

00:09:21.750 --> 00:09:24.060
started forming unions to begin with.

00:09:24.060 --> 00:09:27.030
But unionizing is virtually impossible

00:09:27.030 --> 00:09:28.560
for today's gig workers

00:09:28.560 --> 00:09:31.770
because to form an union
you have to be an employee,

00:09:31.770 --> 00:09:32.940
and people who work for Uber,

00:09:32.940 --> 00:09:35.040
Lyft, Grubhub, Airbnb, and so on

00:09:35.040 --> 00:09:37.860
are not actually employees.

00:09:37.860 --> 00:09:39.690
This may sound like a technicality

00:09:39.690 --> 00:09:42.510
but it's essential to the
way these companies function.

00:09:42.510 --> 00:09:45.060
Unsurprisingly, when faced with a 2020

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:47.730
California ballot initiative, Prop 22,

00:09:47.730 --> 00:09:49.890
which would exempt companies like Uber

00:09:49.890 --> 00:09:52.380
from a state law that would
have required these companies

00:09:52.380 --> 00:09:54.965
to treat workers like formal employees,

00:09:54.965 --> 00:09:58.110
DoorDash, Lyft, Uber,
Instacart, and Postmates

00:09:58.110 --> 00:10:03.110
spent $204 million on a
publicity campaign supporting it.

00:10:03.300 --> 00:10:05.490
See, if somebody is your employee,

00:10:05.490 --> 00:10:08.130
you're obligated to provide
things like overtime,

00:10:08.130 --> 00:10:09.840
bargaining rights and healthcare.

00:10:09.840 --> 00:10:11.700
According to The Seattle Times,

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:13.140
classifying your workforce

00:10:13.140 --> 00:10:15.540
as independent contractors instead

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:19.410
can save companies 30%
or more on labor costs.

00:10:19.410 --> 00:10:21.570
And for companies that were definitely

00:10:21.570 --> 00:10:23.310
not all making a profit,

00:10:23.310 --> 00:10:25.350
this would have made
their tenuous situations

00:10:25.350 --> 00:10:26.400
even more slippery.

00:10:26.400 --> 00:10:29.670
In the end, nearly 60% of voters agreed

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:32.340
that all the bells and
whistles of formal employment

00:10:32.340 --> 00:10:34.980
were unnecessary, especially
if you jacked up prices

00:10:34.980 --> 00:10:37.110
on services they had come to rely on.

00:10:37.110 --> 00:10:39.870
The sheer amount of money
poured into this campaign

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:42.720
made Prop 22 the most
expensive ballot initiative

00:10:42.720 --> 00:10:43.553
in US history,

00:10:43.553 --> 00:10:46.065
and arguably it paid off.

00:10:46.065 --> 00:10:48.420
Except not for workers, though.

00:10:48.420 --> 00:10:50.570
Definitely didn't pay off for them.

00:10:50.570 --> 00:10:52.680
In this way, we can see the gig economy

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:55.080
as being emblematic of a bigger shift

00:10:55.080 --> 00:10:56.550
in the power of labor.

00:10:56.550 --> 00:10:59.797
As sociologist Alexandrea
Ravanelle concluded,

00:10:59.797 --> 00:11:01.800
"For all its app-enabled modernity,

00:11:01.800 --> 00:11:04.770
the gig economy resembles
the early Industrial Age.

00:11:04.770 --> 00:11:07.860
The sharing economy is
truly a movement forward

00:11:07.860 --> 00:11:09.270
to the past."

00:11:09.270 --> 00:11:12.630
That is a past before workers
had all the protections

00:11:12.630 --> 00:11:14.370
they gained through
striking and organizing

00:11:14.370 --> 00:11:15.840
since the 19th century

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:17.940
and through the protections
of the New Deal.

00:11:17.940 --> 00:11:20.160
Now those prospects are unattractive

00:11:20.160 --> 00:11:24.930
to the DoorDashers earning
an average of $1.45 an hour

00:11:24.930 --> 00:11:27.060
for their labor, not counting tips,

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:29.970
according to Seattle-based
advocacy group PayUp.

00:11:29.970 --> 00:11:32.880
But it is hugely attractive
to businesses and investors.

00:11:32.880 --> 00:11:35.640
After all, when you classify
workers as contractors,

00:11:35.640 --> 00:11:38.070
you take a lot less liability.

00:11:38.070 --> 00:11:40.740
Laws governing actual
employment require companies

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:43.770
to implement worker and
consumer safety protections.

00:11:43.770 --> 00:11:44.880
Under the gig economy,

00:11:44.880 --> 00:11:48.690
all that risk is transferred
onto the workers themselves.

00:11:48.690 --> 00:11:50.610
All this while many gig workers

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:53.160
aren't seeing the
flexibility-to-income ratio

00:11:53.160 --> 00:11:54.120
they were promised.

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:56.790
They're urged on by apps
that penalize workers

00:11:56.790 --> 00:11:57.990
who don't work enough,

00:11:57.990 --> 00:12:02.310
like with TaskRabbit's 75%
recommended acceptance rate,

00:12:02.310 --> 00:12:04.710
which impacts the number of
jobs available to workers

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:06.270
as well as the rate.

00:12:06.270 --> 00:12:07.290
As Cherri Murphy,

00:12:07.290 --> 00:12:10.200
a Bay Area-based ride
share driver explains,

00:12:10.200 --> 00:12:13.770
working for Lyft provided
only so-called flexibility

00:12:13.770 --> 00:12:15.000
and not her fellow drivers,

00:12:15.000 --> 00:12:17.280
many of whom also happen
to be people of color,

00:12:17.280 --> 00:12:19.710
were working 70 hours a week

00:12:19.710 --> 00:12:23.130
and still living at or
below the poverty line.

00:12:23.130 --> 00:12:24.457
As Murphy said,

00:12:24.457 --> 00:12:26.340
"Over the years I found myself

00:12:26.340 --> 00:12:28.590
in a deadly and inflexible cycle,

00:12:28.590 --> 00:12:32.220
entrapped in working just
to afford to keep working."

00:12:32.220 --> 00:12:34.590
Ultimately, these gig workers were trapped

00:12:34.590 --> 00:12:36.360
in a big race to the bottom

00:12:36.360 --> 00:12:40.320
plagued by unreliable pay,
alienating working conditions,

00:12:40.320 --> 00:12:43.350
and a profound lack of
institutional support

00:12:43.350 --> 00:12:45.540
from the mega-corporations they work for

00:12:45.540 --> 00:12:47.250
but are not employed by.

00:12:47.250 --> 00:12:50.160
But despite our gripes with
Uber and co's business models,

00:12:50.160 --> 00:12:51.660
we still haven't answered the question,

00:12:51.660 --> 00:12:52.950
why are they struggling?

00:12:52.950 --> 00:12:54.420
We think it's pretty simple,

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:56.280
and it goes deeper than the pandemic

00:12:56.280 --> 00:12:57.420
messed with the economy.

00:12:57.420 --> 00:12:59.460
From the start, these
apps have been indicative

00:12:59.460 --> 00:13:02.790
of an economic logic that
is fundamentally flawed,

00:13:02.790 --> 00:13:05.190
and that's because it's
based on speculation

00:13:05.190 --> 00:13:07.170
about future profitability.

00:13:07.170 --> 00:13:10.290
It's a house built on a foundation of sand

00:13:10.290 --> 00:13:13.140
which was conveniently
delivered by TaskRabbit.

00:13:13.140 --> 00:13:16.440
Gig startups have mixed
material goods and services

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:18.930
carried out by real human beings

00:13:18.930 --> 00:13:20.700
wit algorithm-based apps

00:13:20.700 --> 00:13:23.850
which work on a logic
of inhuman efficiency.

00:13:23.850 --> 00:13:26.610
Just as the 2008 financial
collapse revealed

00:13:26.610 --> 00:13:28.260
that the real estate investment market

00:13:28.260 --> 00:13:30.060
was built upon shaky investments

00:13:30.060 --> 00:13:32.130
and repackaged high-risk loans,

00:13:32.130 --> 00:13:33.480
the snowballing collapse

00:13:33.480 --> 00:13:35.790
of the Uber-for-brushing-your-teeth
industry

00:13:35.790 --> 00:13:38.130
shows that the future of profitability

00:13:38.130 --> 00:13:39.450
that everyone imagined,

00:13:39.450 --> 00:13:43.200
from founders to investors
to workers to consumers,

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:46.920
was exactly that, always rife
with internal contradiction.

00:13:46.920 --> 00:13:50.100
But these companies were
able to speculate so wildly

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:52.860
because they had billions
of dollars of backing

00:13:52.860 --> 00:13:54.150
from venture capitalists.

00:13:54.150 --> 00:13:57.060
It still saved an enormous amount of loss

00:13:57.060 --> 00:14:00.240
by treating its employees like
interchangeable contractors

00:14:00.240 --> 00:14:03.780
rather than actually investing
in a stable workforce.

00:14:03.780 --> 00:14:06.180
What's more, when businesses like Uber

00:14:06.180 --> 00:14:08.070
openly and proudly declare

00:14:08.070 --> 00:14:11.580
that they're here specifically
to disrupt an industry,

00:14:11.580 --> 00:14:13.830
those disruptions can be seismic.

00:14:13.830 --> 00:14:15.210
Many traditional jobs,

00:14:15.210 --> 00:14:18.060
like taxi drivers or hotel concierges,

00:14:18.060 --> 00:14:21.360
have been decimated if
not essentially eradicated

00:14:21.360 --> 00:14:23.130
in a short span of time.

00:14:23.130 --> 00:14:26.790
Most tragically, this has
resulted in a spike in suicide

00:14:26.790 --> 00:14:28.830
amongst New York City taxi drivers

00:14:28.830 --> 00:14:31.410
who, after investing huge sums of money

00:14:31.410 --> 00:14:32.640
to get their medallions

00:14:32.640 --> 00:14:35.070
and huge amounts of time in training,

00:14:35.070 --> 00:14:37.770
saw their livelihoods destroyed.

00:14:37.770 --> 00:14:40.380
Meanwhile, restaurants
adapting to delivery apps

00:14:40.380 --> 00:14:44.460
have seen as much as 33%
commission off of order total.

00:14:44.460 --> 00:14:47.220
So rather than revolutionizing
the modern economy,

00:14:47.220 --> 00:14:49.920
these apps have actually
left it in worse shape

00:14:49.920 --> 00:14:51.060
than they found it,

00:14:51.060 --> 00:14:52.890
and that affects all of us.

00:14:52.890 --> 00:14:53.947
As Hyman writes,

00:14:53.947 --> 00:14:56.430
"When our corporations
fail, it hurts us all.

00:14:56.430 --> 00:14:58.050
Yet when they succeed,

00:14:58.050 --> 00:15:01.710
we do not get compensated for our risks.

00:15:01.710 --> 00:15:04.320
American corporations,
as it is often said,

00:15:04.320 --> 00:15:08.190
socialize risk and privatize return."

00:15:08.190 --> 00:15:09.870
And the architects of these apps

00:15:09.870 --> 00:15:12.600
appear to be getting off
as easily as bankers did

00:15:12.600 --> 00:15:15.210
after the 2008 financial collapse.

00:15:15.210 --> 00:15:17.130
At the same time, for millions of workers,

00:15:17.130 --> 00:15:19.440
protections it took decades of agitation

00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:21.690
by labor unions to be realized

00:15:21.690 --> 00:15:23.430
have been stripped away.

00:15:23.430 --> 00:15:25.890
This whole era calls to mind
something else that happened

00:15:25.890 --> 00:15:27.540
in the mid 20th century.

00:15:27.540 --> 00:15:30.240
The Taft-Hartley Act of 1947,

00:15:30.240 --> 00:15:32.790
which burst in Kool-Aid Man style

00:15:32.790 --> 00:15:34.500
to tamper union practices

00:15:34.500 --> 00:15:36.450
most crucially with the introduction

00:15:36.450 --> 00:15:37.740
of right to work laws,

00:15:37.740 --> 00:15:39.060
which still exist.

00:15:39.060 --> 00:15:41.220
And sure, right to work laws prohibit

00:15:41.220 --> 00:15:42.600
mandatory joining of unions

00:15:42.600 --> 00:15:45.480
for both private and public sector jobs,

00:15:45.480 --> 00:15:48.360
arguably undermining hard-won solidarity.

00:15:48.360 --> 00:15:52.290
As former union buster Martin
Jay Leavitt wrote in 1993,

00:15:52.290 --> 00:15:54.900
Taft-Hartley Act meant "the
bosses could once again

00:15:54.900 --> 00:15:58.140
wage their war with near impunity."

00:15:58.140 --> 00:16:00.690
And at this point, about 14% of US workers

00:16:00.690 --> 00:16:03.450
had already participated
in post-war strikes,

00:16:03.450 --> 00:16:05.490
so bosses were particularly invested

00:16:05.490 --> 00:16:07.440
in shutting that shit down.

00:16:07.440 --> 00:16:09.270
The passing of Taft-Hartley

00:16:09.270 --> 00:16:12.060
effectively defanged
organized labor's power,

00:16:12.060 --> 00:16:13.380
preventing unions from putting

00:16:13.380 --> 00:16:16.590
certain kinds of pressures on employers.

00:16:16.590 --> 00:16:18.630
And the mainstreaming of contractor status

00:16:18.630 --> 00:16:19.770
in the gig economy

00:16:19.770 --> 00:16:21.780
could be seen as yet another attempt

00:16:21.780 --> 00:16:24.240
to systematically roll
back worker protections

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:26.970
and further stymie organized labor.

00:16:26.970 --> 00:16:30.150
And it's one that, if it
continues to be successful,

00:16:30.150 --> 00:16:32.850
could have implications on your work life

00:16:32.850 --> 00:16:35.700
regardless of if you've
ever done gig labor

00:16:35.700 --> 00:16:38.220
because this model seems ripe to expand

00:16:38.220 --> 00:16:41.790
even as the companies that
pioneered it are struggling.

00:16:41.790 --> 00:16:43.110
A recent Mercer study shows

00:16:43.110 --> 00:16:45.600
that 60% of (mumbles) executives

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:47.970
anticipate they will substantially replace

00:16:47.970 --> 00:16:50.310
full-time employees with contract workers

00:16:50.310 --> 00:16:52.170
within the next three years.

00:16:52.170 --> 00:16:56.610
That is to say gig labor may
be coming for a lot of us.

00:16:56.610 --> 00:16:58.440
That would lead many more folks doing

00:16:58.440 --> 00:17:01.470
what Woodcock and Graham
call precarious work,

00:17:01.470 --> 00:17:04.320
i.e. work that comes with "uncertainty

00:17:04.320 --> 00:17:06.090
as to the duration of employment,

00:17:06.090 --> 00:17:08.760
a lack of access to social
protection and benefits

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:10.800
usually associated with employment,

00:17:10.800 --> 00:17:14.160
low pay and substantial
legal and practical obstacles

00:17:14.160 --> 00:17:16.890
to joining a trade union and
bargaining collectively."

00:17:16.890 --> 00:17:18.450
Workers doing this labor

00:17:18.450 --> 00:17:21.480
make up what some call
the precariat class.

00:17:21.480 --> 00:17:23.767
But as scholar Kathleen Thelen points out,

00:17:23.767 --> 00:17:26.250
"Precarity is not the same as poverty

00:17:26.250 --> 00:17:28.740
and it does not only
hit low skill workers.

00:17:28.740 --> 00:17:30.630
It also affects highly skilled groups,

00:17:30.630 --> 00:17:34.020
including professionals such
as airline pilots and lawyers

00:17:34.020 --> 00:17:37.320
who are also employed increasingly
on flexible contracts."

00:17:37.320 --> 00:17:38.910
And hell, there's even a trend

00:17:38.910 --> 00:17:42.060
toward hiring CEOs on short-term basis

00:17:42.060 --> 00:17:44.190
to help companies deal
with sudden departures

00:17:44.190 --> 00:17:45.690
or navigate scandals.

00:17:45.690 --> 00:17:47.970
It seems that truly no one is safe

00:17:47.970 --> 00:17:51.540
from the oncoming shift into
the wild world of gig labor.

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:53.970
From companies hiring
two part-time employees

00:17:53.970 --> 00:17:55.320
instead of one full-timer,

00:17:55.320 --> 00:17:58.290
to outsourcing HR work
to contractors or apps,

00:17:58.290 --> 00:18:01.650
we're increasingly being
taught to expect less and less

00:18:01.650 --> 00:18:02.730
from our employers.

00:18:02.730 --> 00:18:04.350
And they're increasingly aware

00:18:04.350 --> 00:18:05.730
of what they can get away with.

00:18:05.730 --> 00:18:09.187
As former CEO of Gigwalk
Bob Bahramipour put it,

00:18:09.187 --> 00:18:12.900
"You can hire 10,000 people
for 10 to 15 minutes.

00:18:12.900 --> 00:18:16.530
When they're done, those
10,000 people just melt away."

00:18:16.530 --> 00:18:20.640
So are we all fated for a
Frosty the Snowman-style demise?

00:18:20.640 --> 00:18:22.470
Because despite all his criticisms,

00:18:22.470 --> 00:18:24.750
Hyman still believes in
some of the early promises

00:18:24.750 --> 00:18:25.680
of the gig economy.

00:18:25.680 --> 00:18:26.910
He just thinks this version of it

00:18:26.910 --> 00:18:28.500
was deliberately constructed

00:18:28.500 --> 00:18:31.140
to only benefit those at the top.

00:18:31.140 --> 00:18:33.660
But for regular folks
who are still craving

00:18:33.660 --> 00:18:34.770
the freedom of gig work,

00:18:34.770 --> 00:18:38.070
is there a way to make it
actually work for everyone?

00:18:38.070 --> 00:18:41.070
The way Hyman sees it, maybe.

00:18:41.070 --> 00:18:43.920
He argues that the idea
of decentralization

00:18:43.920 --> 00:18:46.650
that underpins the gig or sharing economy

00:18:46.650 --> 00:18:48.180
could usher us into what he calls

00:18:48.180 --> 00:18:51.120
a post-corporate capitalism.

00:18:51.120 --> 00:18:52.920
Worker-owned co-ops could create

00:18:52.920 --> 00:18:54.990
a more dominant peer-to-peer economy

00:18:54.990 --> 00:18:56.640
where apps are owned collectively

00:18:56.640 --> 00:18:58.830
so as to allow workers
to control the fruits

00:18:58.830 --> 00:19:00.240
of their own labor.

00:19:00.240 --> 00:19:02.377
Or as economist David Korten put it,

00:19:02.377 --> 00:19:03.990
"Post-corporate capitalism

00:19:03.990 --> 00:19:05.640
opens up the possibility for us

00:19:05.640 --> 00:19:08.520
to treat money as the facilitator,

00:19:08.520 --> 00:19:11.610
not the purpose of our economic lives."

00:19:11.610 --> 00:19:13.410
And we're already starting to see examples

00:19:13.410 --> 00:19:15.420
of this new approach in real life.

00:19:15.420 --> 00:19:17.460
The Co-Op Ride app, for instance,

00:19:17.460 --> 00:19:19.980
is the largest worker-owned
cooperative in America

00:19:19.980 --> 00:19:22.470
and its members earn up to 10% more

00:19:22.470 --> 00:19:24.720
than when driving for Uber or Lyft.

00:19:24.720 --> 00:19:26.310
After all, if the gig economy

00:19:26.310 --> 00:19:28.440
has weakened traditional corporations

00:19:28.440 --> 00:19:31.860
and opened up our perceptions
of what jobs can be,

00:19:31.860 --> 00:19:35.520
what could that mean for
our relationship to work?

00:19:35.520 --> 00:19:38.160
Is there any way we can get a do-over

00:19:38.160 --> 00:19:40.440
on the promise of the gig economy?

00:19:40.440 --> 00:19:43.800
Hyman writes, "Technology
creates new possibilities,

00:19:43.800 --> 00:19:45.870
but only we determine our future,

00:19:45.870 --> 00:19:49.020
or at least we have every
time before in human history.

00:19:49.020 --> 00:19:52.860
The wage connected to a job
might be a passing moment

00:19:52.860 --> 00:19:54.060
in our economic past,

00:19:54.060 --> 00:19:55.740
a footnote to history."

00:19:55.740 --> 00:19:57.780
Some thinkers have
envisioned a gig economy

00:19:57.780 --> 00:20:00.000
based on what they call portable benefits.

00:20:00.000 --> 00:20:01.950
That is a system where the healthcare

00:20:01.950 --> 00:20:04.860
and paid leave and other
things afforded to employees

00:20:04.860 --> 00:20:07.680
could follow you from
TaskRabbitting Ikea furniture

00:20:07.680 --> 00:20:10.800
to bagging a frat house's
booze supply for Drizly.

00:20:10.800 --> 00:20:14.310
But will this starry-eyed
vision ever manifest?

00:20:14.310 --> 00:20:18.570
All signs are currently
pointing to probably not.

00:20:18.570 --> 00:20:20.760
But at least according to some theorists,

00:20:20.760 --> 00:20:23.460
there is still potential
to use these technologies

00:20:23.460 --> 00:20:25.890
to fundamentally rethink
what labor looks like

00:20:25.890 --> 00:20:27.330
in the first place.

00:20:27.330 --> 00:20:31.350
So what we're saying is
weirder things have happened.

00:20:31.350 --> 00:20:33.030
But what do you all think?

00:20:33.030 --> 00:20:35.250
Can the gig economy be transformed,

00:20:35.250 --> 00:20:38.910
or are we all just gonna
get app-ed to death?

00:20:38.910 --> 00:20:40.320
Let us know in the comments.

00:20:40.320 --> 00:20:42.330
And thanks as always to our
patrons for your support

00:20:42.330 --> 00:20:43.530
and don't forget to check out

00:20:43.530 --> 00:20:45.690
all of our update Patreon perks.

00:20:45.690 --> 00:20:48.780
And check out our WiseCrack
livestream right here

00:20:48.780 --> 00:20:50.640
Thursdays at 11:00 a.m. Pacific.

00:20:50.640 --> 00:20:52.560
Remember to like this video, subscribe,

00:20:52.560 --> 00:20:54.210
and hell, maybe even ring that bell.

00:20:54.210 --> 00:20:57.727
And as always, thanks for watching. Later.

00:20:57.727 --> 00:21:00.310
(upbeat music)

